  "response": "For most of the twentieth century, the homeland of Proto-Indo-European was a question that archaeology and historical linguistics could frame but not close. Colin Renfrew argued that Proto-Indo-European spread with the first farmers from Neolithic Anatolia, carried across Europe by the slow demographic expansion of agriculture. Although he pressed that case with considerable force, Renfrew first set out his Anatolian hypothesis in a 1977 book, long after the competing model had become the standard account. That competitor was the work of Marija Gimbutas, who proposed that Proto-Indo-European spread from the Pontic-Caspian steppe with kurgan-building pastoralists, mobile herders whose burial mounds she traced westward into the Danube basin and beyond. Neither school could dislodge the other, because pottery, burial customs and reconstructed vocabulary could each be read in more than one way. Quantitative linguistics only deepened the deadlock: a 2012 Bayesian phylogeographic study by Bouckaert and colleagues supported an Anatolian origin for the Indo-European language family, modelling the family's dispersal as a diffusion outward from Asia Minor.\n\nAncient DNA changed the terms of the argument by supplying a record of who moved, and when. The Yamnaya culture flourished on the Pontic-Caspian steppe in the late fourth and early third millennia BCE, and provided that this archaeological dating holds, ancient genomes show that a large population movement from the steppe into central Europe occurred around 3000 to 2500 BCE. That movement caused Yamnaya-related herders to contribute most of the ancestry of Corded Ware people, the makers of the cord-impressed pottery and single-grave burials found from the Rhine to the Volga. The majority of Corded Ware genetic ancestry derived from Yamnaya-related populations, an incoming contribution so large that it cannot be explained as gradual intermarriage at a frontier.\n\nThe Corded Ware profile supplied the decisive evidence. On its strength, the Haak study concluded that the Pontic-Caspian steppe was a source for at least some Indo-European languages in Europe, a conclusion the authors had reached before the study was published in the journal Science. That conclusion settles the tension in Renfrew's position: however vigorously the Anatolian farming model was argued, and whenever it was first formulated, a migration on this scale arriving from the east is the population event to which European branches of the family must be tied. The Haak conclusion, moreover, led to the third-millennium steppe expansion into central Europe, since the conclusion is what fixed that expansion as the pivotal episode in the prehistory of the continent's languages.\n\nThe steppe account still had to explain the Anatolian branch, which includes Hittite and is the earliest attested. Here the genomic evidence admits exactly two readings, and one of them must hold: either ancient genomes from Bronze Age Anatolia show steppe-related ancestry above ten percent, or they show steppe-related ancestry of ten percent or less. Whichever is correct, the data bear on the stage before the family divided. At least one of two locations describes the earliest Indo-Anatolian speech community, perhaps both: that community lived partly on the Pontic-Caspian steppe, or it lived partly south of the Caucasus Mountains. Its southern presence led Lazaridis and colleagues to conclude that the Yamnaya population was the common source of the non-Anatolian Indo-European languages, a formulation that keeps the steppe at the centre of the family's later history while placing its deepest roots in the highlands to the south.\n\nThe resulting picture does not simply award victory to one side. The Anatolian farming model fails as an account of how Indo-European languages reached Europe, because the genetic record identifies a later, eastern source for most of that continent's ancestry. Gimbutas's steppe pastoralists emerge as the principal carriers of the European branches. The Anatolian branch, by contrast, points to a more complex origin, and ancient genomes have turned a contest between two homelands into an analysis of how several distinct populations contributed to a single expanding language family.",
